1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,449
A gate swings about its hinge.

2
00:00:02,449 --> 00:00:04,258
A wheel turns on its axle.

3
00:00:04,258 --> 00:00:06,598
A pendulum swings from near its top.

4
00:00:06,998 --> 00:00:11,649
Same kind of object, a different
axis, a different moment of inertia.

5
00:00:12,099 --> 00:00:13,666
Diya spins a coin flat.

6
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:14,901
Then on its edge.

7
00:00:14,901 --> 00:00:16,902
Then on a pin through its rim.

8
00:00:17,252 --> 00:00:19,662
One coin, three spins, three answers.

9
00:00:19,662 --> 00:00:21,990
Must she start from scratch each time?

10
00:00:22,490 --> 00:00:23,390
No.

11
00:00:22,927 --> 00:00:24,340
Two shortcuts do it.

12
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:27,252
The theorems of perpendicular
and parallel axes.

13
00:00:27,852 --> 00:00:30,764
Note: these theorems are not
in the current NCERT book.

14
00:00:30,764 --> 00:00:33,157
They are in the ISC,
JEE and NEET syllabus.

15
00:00:33,507 --> 00:00:37,755
The perpendicular axes theorem works
only for flat bodies, like a coin

16
00:00:37,755 --> 00:00:38,708
or a plate.

17
00:00:39,108 --> 00:00:43,805
The parallel axes theorem works for
any body, as long as one axis passes

18
00:00:43,805 --> 00:00:45,749
through the centre of mass.

19
00:00:46,349 --> 00:00:48,110
First, perpendicular axes.

20
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:49,447
Lay the coin flat.

21
00:00:49,447 --> 00:00:53,670
Draw x and y in its plane, and z
straight up through its centre.

22
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:56,654
A bit of the coin sits at x and y.

23
00:00:56,654 --> 00:01:01,353
Its distance from the z axis is r,
and r squared equals x squared plus

24
00:01:01,353 --> 00:01:02,253
y squared.

25
00:01:02,652 --> 00:01:05,081
Multiply by m and add every bit.

26
00:01:05,081 --> 00:01:07,907
The sum of m r squared splits in two.

27
00:01:08,307 --> 00:01:11,530
Those are I about x, and I about y.

28
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:14,143
So I z equals I x plus I y.

29
00:01:14,743 --> 00:01:20,083
For the coin, I z is half MR squared,
and by symmetry I x equals I y.

30
00:01:20,083 --> 00:01:24,532
So each is a quarter MR squared:
the coin spun on its edge.

31
00:01:25,132 --> 00:01:26,151
Now you try.

32
00:01:26,151 --> 00:01:29,992
A ring has I equal to MR
squared about its central axis.

33
00:01:29,992 --> 00:01:33,259
About a diameter, is
it MR squared, or half?

34
00:01:33,259 --> 00:01:34,526
Pause and decide.

35
00:01:37,626 --> 00:01:38,816
Half MR squared!

36
00:01:38,816 --> 00:01:41,274
The two diameters share the total equally.

37
00:01:41,874 --> 00:01:44,813
Now parallel axes, on our motion notebook.

38
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:49,698
A two kilogram disc, radius point two
metres, spinning about its centre.

39
00:01:49,698 --> 00:01:51,336
I is point zero four.

40
00:01:51,686 --> 00:01:54,950
Move the axis point one
metres away from the centre.

41
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:58,423
Now the centre of the disc
goes round in a circle too.

42
00:01:58,823 --> 00:01:59,874
Slow-mo replay.

43
00:01:59,874 --> 00:02:00,774
Freeze.

44
00:02:00,873 --> 00:02:02,752
The motion has two parts.

45
00:02:02,752 --> 00:02:07,062
The centre goes round the axis,
like a mass M at distance d.

46
00:02:07,062 --> 00:02:08,784
That adds M d squared.

47
00:02:09,184 --> 00:02:13,071
And it still turns about its
own centre, keeping I c m.

48
00:02:13,071 --> 00:02:14,790
Total: point zero six.

49
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:17,341
Slide the axis to the rim.

50
00:02:17,341 --> 00:02:22,520
d is point two, M d squared is point
zero eight, and I is point one two.

51
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,042
Three times as much.

52
00:02:24,442 --> 00:02:27,888
On the graph, I against d
squared is a straight line.

53
00:02:27,888 --> 00:02:31,062
It starts at I c m, and
its slope is the mass.

54
00:02:31,662 --> 00:02:33,116
Why does this work?

55
00:02:33,116 --> 00:02:34,206
Step by step.

56
00:02:34,556 --> 00:02:35,456
Step one.

57
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:38,448
Measure each bit from the
centre of mass with r.

58
00:02:38,448 --> 00:02:41,461
From the new axis, its
distance is r plus d.

59
00:02:42,211 --> 00:02:43,111
Step two.

60
00:02:43,031 --> 00:02:44,420
Square it and add.

61
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:49,359
You get the sum of m r squared, plus
two d times the sum of m r, plus M

62
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:50,259
d squared.

63
00:02:50,979 --> 00:02:51,879
Step three.

64
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:55,672
Measured from the centre of
mass, the sum of m r is zero.

65
00:02:55,672 --> 00:02:57,539
So the middle part vanishes.

66
00:02:58,289 --> 00:02:59,189
Step four.

67
00:02:59,159 --> 00:03:02,020
What is left is I c m plus M d squared.

68
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:04,031
Remember this.

69
00:03:04,031 --> 00:03:07,991
Parallel axes: I equals
I c m plus M d squared.

70
00:03:07,991 --> 00:03:13,400
Perpendicular axes: I z equals I
x plus I y, for flat bodies only.

71
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:15,145
Side by side.

72
00:03:15,145 --> 00:03:18,793
A thin rod about its middle
has ML squared over twelve.

73
00:03:19,193 --> 00:03:22,453
Shift the axis to its
end, half a length away.

74
00:03:22,453 --> 00:03:24,830
Add M times L over two, squared.

75
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:28,291
That is ML squared over
three, four times bigger.

76
00:03:28,291 --> 00:03:31,771
That is why the ruler was
hard to spin by its end.

77
00:03:32,371 --> 00:03:33,342
Now you try.

78
00:03:33,342 --> 00:03:37,855
Of all the parallel axes through a body,
which one gives the smallest moment

79
00:03:37,855 --> 00:03:38,755
of inertia?

80
00:03:38,722 --> 00:03:39,927
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:43,127 --> 00:03:45,569
The one through the centre of mass!

82
00:03:45,569 --> 00:03:48,333
Any other axis adds M d squared on top.

83
00:03:48,983 --> 00:03:50,672
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:54,218
A two kilogram disc of
radius point two metres.

85
00:03:54,218 --> 00:03:56,793
Find I about a tangent in its plane.

86
00:03:57,293 --> 00:03:59,244
First, perpendicular axes.

87
00:03:59,244 --> 00:04:02,761
About a diameter, I is
half of point zero four.

88
00:04:02,761 --> 00:04:04,018
Point zero two.

89
00:04:04,768 --> 00:04:08,374
The tangent is parallel
to it, a distance R away.

90
00:04:08,374 --> 00:04:11,597
Add MR squared: two times point zero four.

91
00:04:11,597 --> 00:04:12,954
Point zero eight.

92
00:04:13,704 --> 00:04:16,791
So I is point zero two
plus point zero eight.

93
00:04:16,791 --> 00:04:18,981
Point one kilogram metre squared.

94
00:04:19,631 --> 00:04:21,093
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:21,443 --> 00:04:25,539
Many students add M d squared
between any two parallel axes.

96
00:04:25,939 --> 00:04:29,122
But one axis must pass
through the centre of mass.

97
00:04:29,122 --> 00:04:32,933
Between two other axes, go
through the centre of mass first.

98
00:04:33,583 --> 00:04:35,165
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:35,165 --> 00:04:40,803
In finding I about an edge, a corner or
a tangent, using one or both theorems.

100
00:04:41,403 --> 00:04:42,682
Exam level now.

101
00:04:42,682 --> 00:04:47,949
A uniform square plate, mass M, side
a, has I equal to M a squared over

102
00:04:47,949 --> 00:04:51,526
six about its central axis
at right angles to it.

103
00:04:51,526 --> 00:04:53,423
Find I about a diagonal.

104
00:04:53,423 --> 00:04:54,756
Pause and solve.

105
00:05:00,156 --> 00:05:01,532
The answer is B.

106
00:05:01,532 --> 00:05:03,399
M a squared over twelve.

107
00:05:03,899 --> 00:05:08,243
The two diagonals cross at right angles
in the plate, through its centre.

108
00:05:08,743 --> 00:05:13,790
So by perpendicular axes, the two
diagonal values add up to M a squared

109
00:05:13,790 --> 00:05:14,702
over six.

110
00:05:15,202 --> 00:05:17,008
By symmetry they are equal.

111
00:05:17,008 --> 00:05:19,364
So each is M a squared over twelve.

112
00:05:19,964 --> 00:05:21,504
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:21,504 --> 00:05:23,087
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:26,287 --> 00:05:28,226
Can you answer these without help?

115
00:05:28,626 --> 00:05:29,526
One.

116
00:05:29,052 --> 00:05:32,244
Why must one of the axes pass
through the centre of mass?

117
00:05:33,144 --> 00:05:34,044
Two.

118
00:05:33,589 --> 00:05:37,259
Why does the perpendicular axes
theorem work only for flat bodies?

119
00:05:38,159 --> 00:05:39,059
Three.

120
00:05:38,754 --> 00:05:42,963
Which parallel axis gives the
smallest moment of inertia, and why?

121
00:05:43,863 --> 00:05:47,781
Next time: a potter keeps a heavy
wheel turning with one foot.

122
00:05:47,781 --> 00:05:49,914
How much work does each push do?

123
00:05:49,914 --> 00:05:52,601
Torque, work and
rotational kinetic energy.
